Let $M$ be a compact two-dimensional manifold of bounded curvature in the sense of Aleksandrov, i.e., a two-dimensional manifold equipped with an intrinsic metric $\rho$ such that there exists a sequence of $C^\infty$ Riemannian metrics $\rho_n$ on $M$ which converge uniformly to $\rho$ on $M$ and whose total absolute (integral) Gaussian curvatures $\int_M |K_n|\, dA_n$ are uniformly bounded. A point $p \in M$ is called a peak point if the angle measure at $p$ in the metric $\rho$ equals $0$ (equivalently, the curvature concentrated at $p$ equals $2\pi$). Assume $M$ has no peak points.

A finite quasi-isometric chart on $M$ is a finite family $\{(U_i, \varphi_i)\}_{i=1}^N$ such that:
(i) each $U_i \subset M$ is a domain homeomorphic to an open disk and $\bigcup_{i=1}^N U_i = M$;
(ii) each map $\varphi_i : U_i \to V_i \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ is a quasi-isometric homeomorphism onto a planar domain $V_i$, meaning there is a constant $L_i \ge 1$ with $L_i^{-1} \rho(x,y) \le \|\varphi_i(x) - \varphi_i(y)\| \le L_i \rho(x,y)$ for all $x,y \in U_i$;
(iii) for every $i,j$ with $U_i \cap U_j \neq \emptyset$, the coordinate transition $\varphi_j \circ \varphi_i^{-1} : \varphi_i(U_i \cap U_j) \to \varphi_j(U_i \cap U_j)$ is a quasi-isometric homeomorphism between the corresponding planar open sets.

Question: Does every such $M$ admit a finite quasi-isometric chart?

